---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}
const props = Astro.props;
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      A color format is just a different way of writing the same color. A screen mixes red, green,
      and blue light to make every pixel, and each format describes that mix from a different angle:
      HEX and RGB name the raw channels, HSL and HSV describe hue and brightness, OKLCH describes how
      light a color actually looks, and CMYK approximates the inks a printer would use. Converting
      between them lets you copy the exact value the tool in front of you expects.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-formats">
    <h2>What each format encodes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>HEX</strong> writes the red, green, and blue channels as two hexadecimal digits each, so #3366ff is 51 red, 102 green, 255 blue. An optional pair on the end sets alpha.</li>
      <li><strong>RGB</strong> writes those same channels as plain numbers from 0 to 255, which is easier to read and edit by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>HSL</strong> describes a color by hue (an angle on the color wheel), saturation, and lightness, so nudging the hue is one number away.</li>
      <li><strong>HSV</strong> is close to HSL but swaps lightness for value, which many color pickers use internally.</li>
      <li><strong>OKLCH</strong> uses lightness, chroma, and hue in a perceptually uniform space, so equal lightness looks equally bright to the eye.</li>
      <li><strong>CMYK</strong> estimates the cyan, magenta, yellow, and black inks a printer would combine, as a screen-based approximation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to">
    <h2>How to convert a color</h2>
    <p>
      Type or paste any color into the input. You can use a hex code with or without the hash, an
      rgb() or hsl() function, or a common CSS name like teal. Every format updates the moment you
      type, and the picker beside the field lets you drag to a new color instead. Press the copy
      button on any row to grab that single value, or use Copy all to take the whole set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hsl-vs-oklch">
    <h2>When to reach for OKLCH over HSL</h2>
    <p>
      HSL is convenient but its lightness is not perceptual: a fully saturated yellow at 50 percent
      lightness looks far brighter than a blue at the same number. That is why HSL shade scales often
      have a muddy or uneven step. OKLCH fixes this because its lightness matches what your eye sees,
      so a scale built by holding chroma and hue steady while stepping lightness produces even,
      predictable shades. If you are generating design tokens or gradients, prefer OKLCH.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="print-note">
    <h2>A note on CMYK and print</h2>
    <p>
      The CMYK value here is a direct formula from sRGB, the color space screens use. Real printing
      depends on the specific inks, paper stock, and ICC profile of your press, so the same CMYK
      numbers can look different on different machines. Use this value as a quick reference, and ask
      your printer for their profile when color accuracy matters.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Private by design</h2>
    <p>
      Every conversion happens in your browser. No color you enter is uploaded or stored on a server,
      and the tool keeps working with no network once the page has loaded, so you can install it and
      use it offline.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
